  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(\\frac{9}{2},1)$ and passes through the point $(4,0).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(\\frac{9}{2},1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}=4p(x-\\frac{9}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(4,0),$ we substitute $x=4$ and $y=0$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-1)^{2} & =4p(4-\\frac{9}{2}) \\\\ 1 & =4p(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2}=-2(x-\\frac{9}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =(0+9) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-2,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-2$ and $4.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
